-\paragraph{Limitation 2: Recovery speed alone omits retention value and
-resource costs.}
-A branch that can tolerate recovery delay may still reuse its history
-on the next normal request. Removing its last KV copy forfeits that reuse;
-moving the copy to a slower tier preserves it but adds retrieval work.
-Conversely, keeping every recoverable history in fast memory consumes
-space needed by other histories and foreground execution. Minimizing
-recovery latency in isolation therefore does not resolve which state
-to retain, where to retain it, or how much protection the serving system
-can afford.
-
-\paragraph{Challenge 2: Balancing fast recovery, reuse and resource use.}
-Finite capacity prevents every long history from remaining near
-execution. Protection must decide both which histories retain reusable
-state and which state merits faster access. These decisions
-need to combine the workflow impact of recovery with future normal reuse,
-capacity occupancy and data movement. In particular, low recovery
-urgency does not imply low reuse value, and a faster restore path is
-useful only to the extent that it advances required work. The challenge
-is to allocate protection resources as these demands change without
-allowing protection itself to undermine foreground service.
+The tier costs in Figure~\ref{fig:motivation-recompute} make the resulting
+allocation tradeoff concrete. Retaining KV in a slower tier already
+avoids much of the full-prefill cost; faster tiers reduce the remaining
+retrieval delay at a higher capacity cost. Whether that reduction
+advances workflow completion depends on the recovering branch's slack.
+Conversely, a branch with ample slack may still reuse its history during
+normal execution, so low recovery urgency does not justify deleting its
+last reusable copy. These are distinct reasons to retain and accelerate
+state, even when both use the same storage hierarchy.
+
+\paragraph{Challenge 2: Allocating KV protection across storage tiers.}
+A protection policy must decide both which histories remain recoverable
+from saved KV and which merit faster access, accounting for workflow
+recovery urgency, normal reuse and tier capacity costs. These demands
+change as agents accumulate context, invoke tools and release dependent
+work. The policy must preserve useful state outside the protected
+failure boundary while limiting the capacity and copying work taken
+from foreground service. Simply adding checkpoints to a reuse-oriented
+cache does not determine these priorities; managing the two objectives
+requires a coordinated retention and placement policy.
 
